// training-crouch-slide.map — Straf3 training stub, "Lintel"
//
// Valve 220 source. This file is the SINGLE source of the geometry:
// straf3-map compiles it to both the collision hulls and the render mesh, so
// the thing you see and the thing you hit cannot drift apart. Edit it as a
// .map (TrenchBroom, Radiant) — do not mirror these numbers into code.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THIS IS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT
//
// A training stub in the sense of VISION §6: it isolates one movement
// primitive — the crouch slide — and teaches nothing else. It is NOT a course.
//
// It exists because `assets/maps/coil.map` cannot present this mechanic at
// all. coil's own header, line 9: "No BSP and no PVS, so the world does not
// need to be sealed and there are no ceilings; brushes exist only where the
// player can reach them." The crouch slide's whole stated value, from
// `PhysicsProfile::experimental`, is that "carrying speed under a low ceiling
// is worth doing" — and on a map with no ceilings anywhere it degenerates into
// a friction toggle on open ground, which is the exact thing its design set out
// to avoid. So the operator cannot judge it on coil, whatever the verdict.
//
// A stub answers "can I do this, and does it feel good in isolation". It does
// NOT answer "does this compose inside a real course", which VISION §7 calls
// the harder and more important question — "movement mechanics cannot be
// evaluated independently from the spaces in which those mechanics are used".
// Do not read a good time here as evidence that the mechanic belongs in canon.
// Read it as evidence about the primitive alone.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STATUS — what is done and what is not
//
// DONE:
//   - Geometry, entities, spawn, start/finish triggers and two checkpoints:
//     complete. This is a playable, timeable map.
//   - Both lines the mechanic admits are present and comparable in one run —
//     see "THE TWO LINES" below. That was a deliberate build requirement, not
//     an accident of the shape.
//   - Every dimension below is derived from a named constant, and the
//     derivation is written out, so a retune is a tuning pass and not a
//     redesign. See "WHICH DIMENSION DEPENDS ON WHICH CONSTANT".
//
// NOT DONE:
//   - The speed thresholds in S3/S5 marked "Predicted:" are arithmetic from a
//     continuous friction model, NOT measurements from the real mover. coil's
//     header earns the word "Measured:" and this one does not yet. The real
//     integrator is sub-stepped and discrete and the slide timer decrements per
//     sub-step, so expect these to move by a few per cent. Whoever measures
//     them should replace "Predicted:" with "Measured:" and correct the number.
//   - No wall-jump stub. That is the other half of r26 and it is not built.
//   - Never played by a human. Never run by a bot. Compilation and geometry
//     were checked; the route has not been driven end to end.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PHYSICS THIS WAS BUILT AGAINST
//
// Canon (`PhysicsProfile::cpm`, id-verified): gravity 800, jump 270, step 18,
// friction 6, stop_speed 100, max_speed 320, duck_scale 0.25,
// min_walk_normal 0.7. Standing hull mins.z −24, maxs.z +32; crouched
// maxs.z +16. A jump lasts 0.675 s flat and peaks 45.6 units.
//
// Candidate (`PhysicsProfile::experimental` only — all zero in canon):
// slide_entry_speed 400, slide_friction 1.0, slide_duration_ms 600.
//
// "ups" below is horizontal speed in units/second.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ONE NUMBER THIS MAP IS ABOUT: z = 48
//
// A player resting on a floor whose top is z=0 has their origin at z=24.125 —
// 24 for `hull_mins.z` and an eighth for `SURFACE_CLIP_EPSILON`, the distance
// the mover holds the hull off a surface. Crouching lowers the top of the hull
// and leaves the underside alone (`PM_CheckDuck`), so:
//
//     standing head    24.125 + 32 = 56.125
//     crouched head    24.125 + 16 = 40.125
//
// A ceiling admits the crouched hull and refuses the standing one only in the
// open band (40.125, 56.125). Its midpoint is 48.125. This map uses **48** —
// an eighth below the exact midpoint, because 48 is on the grid and the eighth
// is `SURFACE_CLIP_EPSILON` showing up in the player's resting height rather
// than anything worth chasing. Margins: the crouched hull clears by 7.875
// units, the standing hull is refused by 8.125. Symmetric to a sixteenth.
//
// `crates/straf3-collision/src/testbed.rs`'s `ceiling_at` picks 48 for the same
// band and its own test says why 20 would be wrong: a crouched player is still
// 40 units tall, so "low enough to crouch under" is not "low". This map is that
// fixture's intent in playable geometry, deliberately not its code.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE LINE, AND WHY EACH PIECE IS THE SHAPE IT IS
//
//   S0  y −768..0     Run-up. 768 units of corridor before the clock starts,
//                     so the clock measures the technique rather than the
//                     acceleration. Spawn in the south at angle 90 (+Y).
//
//   S1  y 0..2048     Strafe corridor, 448 wide, 2048 long. Pure acceleration,
//                     and it exists only to clear `slide_entry_speed`.
//                     448 is coil's corridor width; 2048 is a third longer than
//                     coil's 1536 because coil enters its corridor already fast
//                     off a circle jump and this map starts from a standstill.
//                     Checkpoint 1 at the far end, so the split before it is
//                     "how fast did I arrive" and the split after it is the
//                     mechanic.
//
//   S2  y 2048..2240  Entry pad. 192 flat units at full height. This is where
//                     the slide is armed, and it has to be flat and it has to
//                     be here: `check_slide` arms on the *crouch press edge*
//                     and reads horizontal speed at that instant, and
//                     `step.rs` zeroes `slide_ms` the moment the player leaves
//                     the ground ("a slide banked across a jump is a different
//                     and worse mechanic"). So the player must LAND, and press
//                     crouch while grounded, at 400 ups or better. 192 units is
//                     roughly one bunnyhop cycle at 550 ups — long enough to
//                     land in, short enough that dawdling costs the clock.
//
//   S3  y 2240..2496  THE LINTEL. The ceiling arrives as a 1:1.33 soffit
//                     descending from z=96 to z=48 over y 2240..2304, then runs
//                     flat at z=48 for 192 units to y=2496.
//
//                     The soffit is a slope and not a lintel face on purpose,
//                     for two reasons. (1) A player who failed to crouch meets
//                     a wedge and is squeezed to a stop rather than slamming a
//                     wall — legible, recoverable, costs seconds not the run;
//                     they crouch where they stand and carry on at 80 ups.
//                     (2) A vertical face here would have normal z = 0 and
//                     would therefore be a **wall-jump surface** under
//                     `experimental` (`wall_normal_max` 0.3), which would let
//                     a second candidate mechanic leak into a stub built to
//                     isolate one. The soffit's normal has |z| = 0.8, five
//                     hundredths of a unit clear of `min_walk_normal` in the
//                     other direction and nowhere near 0.3.
//
//                     Standing is actually refused from y≈2293.2, where the
//                     soffit passes below 56.125 — so the refused run is 202.8
//                     units, not the flat 192.
//
//                     Predicted: friction 1.0 decays speed as e^(−t) with t in
//                     seconds, so a slide entered at v carries v·(1−e^(−0.6))
//                     = 0.4512·v units before the 600 ms runs out. 202.8 units
//                     therefore needs about 450 ups to span on the slide alone,
//                     and a bare-minimum 400 ups entry runs out ~22 units
//                     inside. That gap between "may enter" (400) and "will get
//                     through" (~450) is the point of the section: the entry
//                     speed is a licence, not a pass.
//
//   S4  y 2496..3264  Exit run. 768 open units at full height. Checkpoint 2 is
//                     at the lintel's mouth so this split is exactly "what did
//                     I leave the tunnel with". A good slide exits here with
//                     roughly 230 ms still on `slide_ms` at ~410 ups; a
//                     crouch-walk exits at 80.
//
//   S5  y 3264..3296  Finish, then 224 units of run-out so an overshoot at
//                     speed still lands on floor.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE TWO LINES — and why this map presents both
//
// `PM_Friction` reads `p.timers.slide_ms`, **not `p.crouched`**. That is not
// obvious from anywhere a map author would look, and it changes what this map
// is testing. Consequence: a player can tap crouch to arm the slide, stand
// straight back up, and keep one-sixth friction for the full 600 ms **at full
// wish speed** — because standing restores the 320 wish speed that
// `duck_scale` 0.25 was suppressing to 80.
//
//   Line A, stay crouched:  arm on the entry pad, hold crouch, slide the
//                           lintel, exit. The mechanic as its designer
//                           described it.
//   Line B, tap and stand:  arm on the entry pad, release crouch, run the open
//                           ground at 320 wish speed under 1.0 friction. Cannot
//                           pass the lintel — `check_duck` refuses to stand
//                           where there is no headroom, so under the lintel B
//                           collapses into A.
//
// So the geometry makes them meet: the entry pad (S2) and the 768-unit exit run
// (S4) are both open ground where B is available and strictly better, and the
// lintel between them is 202.8 units where only A exists. A player who burns
// the whole slide on B before the lintel crouch-walks the tunnel at 80 ups and
// loses about two seconds. A player who saves it for A gets through and still
// has ~230 ms of B on the far side. Both lines are in one run, on one clock.
//
// **Whether B dominates A is UNMEASURED.** It is the single most likely thing
// to decide crouch slide's verdict in a later wave, and no sweep in this tree
// has answered it. `docs/movement-canon.md` records the mechanism; this map is
// where it can be felt. If B does dominate, then a low ceiling is not what the
// mechanic is *for* in play, and this map's central premise needs revisiting
// rather than its dimensions.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHICH DIMENSION DEPENDS ON WHICH CONSTANT
//
// The candidate constants are opening positions, not tuned values. If one
// moves, this is what moves with it:
//
//   hull_maxs.z (32) and crouched_height (16)
//       → the ceiling height 48, and ONLY that. Both are id-verified and will
//         not move. If they ever do, 48 becomes the midpoint of the new
//         (−hull_mins.z + crouched_height + ε, −hull_mins.z + hull_maxs.z + ε)
//         band, rounded to the grid.
//
//   slide_entry_speed (400)
//       → the length of S1, the strafe corridor (2048). It must be long enough
//         to strafejump past this from a standstill. Raise the constant, extend
//         the corridor. Nothing else in the map reads it.
//
//   slide_duration_ms (600) and slide_friction (1.0)
//       → the length of the lintel (192 flat / 202.8 refused) and of the exit
//         run (768). Together they set the slide distance 0.4512·v. Shorten the
//         duration or raise the friction and the lintel must shorten in step,
//         or the section stops being crossable on a slide and starts teaching
//         the wrong thing.
//
//   wall_normal_max (0.3)
//       → the soffit's slope, and nothing else. |normal.z| = 0.8 there, so this
//         constant would have to more than double before the soffit became a
//         wall. The corridor walls are a different matter — see below.
//
//   gravity (800), jump_velocity (270)
//       → nothing. There is no jump in this map that has to clear anything.
//         That is deliberate: a stub isolates one primitive.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// AN HONEST NOTE ABOUT WALLS
//
// Every corridor wall in this map is vertical, so |normal.z| = 0 and every one
// of them is a **wall-jump surface** under `experimental`. That is true of
// coil's corridor walls too. The operator decision that brought this map into
// scope says coil "cannot reward wall jump", which is exactly right and is not
// the same claim as "coil has no wall-jump surfaces" — it has plenty; it has
// nowhere that pushing off one is the answer. Neither does this map. Nothing
// here needs a wall jump and nothing here is faster with one, so the primitive
// stays isolated in practice even though the surfaces exist.
//
// The surface families are named for what the simulation does with them, as in
// coil: straf3/floor and straf3/pad are walkable, straf3/wall is vertical,
// straf3/lintel is the low ceiling. The NAME decides nothing — the compiler
// reads the plane normal, and `texture.rs` gives none of these any special
// meaning. The names are colour and legibility only.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
